refs += [dict(id='optimization',title='A Structural Investigation of the Approximability of Polynomial-Time Problems',authors='Karl Bringmann; Alejandro Cassis; Nick Fischer; Marvin Künnemann',year=2022,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2022.30',pdf_url='https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol229-icalp2022/LIPIcs.ICALP.2022.30/LIPIcs.ICALP.2022.30.pdf',locator='Section 3.2, discussion preceding Theorem 8; distinguishes the proved degree-hardness optimization equivalence from the open decision model-checking analogue; full version arXiv:2204.11681.')]
notes=['Recovered the conditional implication, including fast detection for every clique size at a fixed hyperedge arity.','Defined the source’s restriction-based hardness parameter, including the universal choice of free-variable set and existential choice of fixed values.','Recovered explicitly partite undirected graphs and fixed-formula data complexity; no unrestricted first-order or unpartite replacement.','Used a common positive premise exponent saving as in the displayed source question; conclusion savings and algorithms may depend on each fixed formula.','Distinguished the 2022 exact-optimization theorem using polynomial-degree hardness from this decision question using restriction hardness.']
save(i,dict(title='Hyperclique speedups for graph properties of fixed hardness',formal='For every fixed integer h≥3, does the following implication hold? Assume that there exists δ∈(0,1) such that, for every fixed integer r≥h+1, an h-uniform hypergraph on N vertices can be tested for an r-vertex clique in worst-case time O(N^{r−δ}). Then, for every fixed k≥h and every fixed ∃ᵏ∀ graph sentence ψ of hardness H(ψ)=h defined below, is there an ε_ψ>0 and an algorithm deciding MC(ψ) in worst-case time O(m^{k−ε_ψ})? All algorithms in this statement are deterministic in the RAM model below. The premise assumes speedups for all clique sizes at one fixed hyperedge arity; it is not the unqualified assertion that these speedups exist.',definitions='The input to MC(ψ) is an explicitly represented finite simple undirected graph G with a partition V=X₁⊔⋯⊔X_k⊔Y. Every part is listed, may be empty, and edges join different parts. The fixed sentence is ψ=(∃x₁∈X₁)⋯(∃x_k∈X_k)(∀y∈Y)φ(x₁,…,x_k,y), where φ is a Boolean formula whose atoms are E(u,v) for distinct variable names u,v among x₁,…,x_k,y. E is symmetric adjacency; equality, extra relations and quantifier alternation inside φ are absent. A universal quantifier over empty Y is true and an existential quantifier over an empty X_i is false. The formula and k are fixed outside the input. Let m=1+|V|+|E|, counting vertex/part-membership records as well as edges; this makes the sparse input-size convention explicit and includes the cost of listed isolated vertices. To define hardness, set every atom E(x_i,x_j) to false in φ and replace E(x_i,y)=E(y,x_i) by z_i. The resulting Boolean function f:{0,1}ᵏ→{0,1} determines H(ψ). For a nonconstant f, H(f) is the largest t∈{0,…,k} such that, for every I⊆[k] of size k−t, there exists an assignment a∈{0,1}ᴵ for which the restricted function on the remaining t variables has exactly one falsifying assignment. Put H(f)=0 if f is constant, and H(ψ)=H(f). Thus the free coordinates must work for every choice of their set, but the pinned values may depend on that set. An h-uniform hypergraph has a finite vertex set and a set of unordered h-element hyperedges. An r-clique is a set of r distinct vertices every h-element subset of which is a hyperedge. The premise uses N, the number of hypergraph vertices, and an explicit adjacency table or list with polynomial-time conversion for fixed h. Use deterministic random-access machines with O(log L)-bit words for input-size parameter L, unit-cost arithmetic, comparisons, Boolean operations, shifts and indirect access on a constant number of words; arbitrarily long integers do not have unit cost. In the premise L may be taken as N for fixed h,r; in the conclusion it is m. Input encodings use vertex numbers and explicit edges and parts. Machines are uniform in input size, with no length-dependent advice; a different fixed machine and multiplicative constant may be used for each fixed r or ψ. The same δ works for all r in the premise. ε_ψ and its constants may depend on h,ψ and the assumed speedup, but not on m. This common-δ reading retains the premise’s displayed exponent saving rather than replacing it by a single fast clique size.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the displayed implication for every fixed h≥3, with a positive exponent saving for every fixed graph sentence of that hardness, or rigorously refute the implication. The known reverse-direction hardness result, an example of one easy sentence, or a speedup for only some sentences does not establish the universal conclusion. A theorem for optimizing the number of satisfying universal-variable choices under a different hardness parameter does not by itself answer the decision target.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The source supplies one direction of the connection',text='For a sentence of hardness h≥3, Theorem 3 derives the absence of a polynomial exponent saving from the h-uniform HyperClique hypothesis. The open question asks whether suitable hyperclique speedups are also sufficient for all sentences at that hardness level.',citation='primary'),dict(title='An existential equivalence is already known',text='Proposition 4 gives some k>h and some sentence of hardness h whose exponent can improve exactly when the corresponding hyperclique hypothesis fails. It does not cover every sentence, which is the additional quantifier in the present question.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Partitioned quantifiers are part of the problem',text='The source explicitly warns that allowing every quantified variable to range over the entire graph can turn some of its hard properties into easy ones. Each variable therefore retains its own input part here.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Later optimization equivalence uses a different parameter',text='The 2022 paper proves an analogue for exact optimization using degree hardness of the polynomial representing a Boolean predicate. It explicitly distinguishes the open decision model-checking analogue. That degree is not the restriction-based H(ψ) in this card.',citation='optimization')],why='Would determine whether hyperclique detection exactly controls the algorithmic power needed at each structural hardness level of this expressive family of polynomial-time graph queries.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='The source establishes hardness levels, algorithmic results for low hardness and an existential equivalence; its final question asks for the universal speedup transfer.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022',text='An exact-optimization analogue is proved using degree hardness; the authors distinguish the remaining decision model-checking question.',citation='optimization')],status='open',status_note='Read the entire source question, its premise, the hardness definition, sparse partite model and preceding proposition. Checked the 2022 follow-up and full version, which distinguish their optimization result from this decision analogue. Later searches through 12 September 2026 found no proof or refutation of the stated universal implication. Searches are not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Restored the missing hyperclique premise and source-specific hardness definition; explicitly states the common-δ premise and formula-dependent conclusion rather than claiming a broader quantifier equivalence.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A potential exact structural classification of fine-grained graph-query complexity at every hardness level, beyond existing one-way reductions and an existential example. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The source classifies fixed ∃ᵏ∀ queries on explicitly partitioned graphs by a restriction-based Boolean hardness parameter.','It proves conditional lower bounds from hyperclique detection at matching hyperedge arity.','The remaining question asks whether fast hyperclique detection for all clique sizes also accelerates every graph query of that hardness.','An equivalence for at least one query at each level is already known.','The 2022 optimization analogue uses a different hardness parameter and does not settle the universal decision question.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','optimization'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11681','https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2026.94'])
